<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Benin</h1><p class="st-meta">1960 CE – present · capital Porto-Novo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A West African republic, independent from France in 1960 as Dahomey, which was a Marxist-Leninist state from 1975 to 1990 and then a multi-party democracy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-dahomey">French Dahomey</a> was granted autonomy as the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> in 1958 and became fully independent on 1 August 1960, now Independence Day. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubert-maga">Hubert Maga</a> was the president who led the country to independence. In 1975 it was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bight-of-benin">Bight of Benin</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After 1960 coups and regime changes followed, dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubert-maga">Hubert Maga</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sourou-migan-apithy">Sourou Migan Apithy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-ahomadegbe-tometin">Justin Ahomadégbé</a> and Émile Derlin Zinsou. On 26 October 1972 Lt. Col. Mathieu Kérékou seized power, and in 1974 he declared the country officially Marxist. It was renamed the People's Republic of Benin on 30 November 1975. Economic collapse and riots in 1989 led Kérékou to renounce Marxism, and the country became the Republic of Benin on 1 March 1990. Kérékou lost the 1991 election to Nicéphore Soglo, and returned to power after winning in 1996.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Thomas Boni Yayi won the presidency in 2006, and Patrice Talon took office on 6 April 2016 and was re-elected in April 2021. On 7 December 2025 a faction of the army claimed to have overthrown Talon, but the government said it had suppressed the coup. A constitutional reform adopted in November 2025 created a Senate, which was sworn in on 30 July 2026.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/benin.jpg" alt="Benin" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Drawn by SKopp, rewritten by Gabbe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Benin.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1960 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Porto-Novo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hubert Maga, Mathieu Kérékou, Nicéphore Soglo, Thomas Boni Yayi, Patrice Talon</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Islam, traditional faiths</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Fon, Bariba, Yoruba, Dendi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/benin">Benin</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Dahomey, Dahomey, People's Republic of Benin</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=benin" aria-label="Benin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Benin lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g00000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5222 4447L5221 4448"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5221 4448h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5222 4447h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5222 4447h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Porto-Novo. <a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=benin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
